83 #uuid "cfddd069-ef87-5e55-83af-2dcabd0224f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375318 #edb/ref 1080 "images/06/062003a83f4afa0cadd68eee54dc0379e469041f3fd0b2371acf5428d8709306.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375318 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375318 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e0121ce2-27fc-58d8-947a-5788ad384bcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375319 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ef/ef9d0a612a093852fd9f60fb20c86b946c178f9e2a9877ef7c16f89f98e324f6.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375319 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375319 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d74f904-81a5-542a-b32a-e0709ccd1fed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375320 #edb/ref 1080 "images/67/677097b290404e318a097ee79fba82a2f3217ce2762df983229b75ef3d493f13.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375320 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375320 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3631542a-0a0a-5e75-9d2a-b39d672ba7dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375328 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/88/8861ad010cbd19d897ace4c8da2fdaeca25e9d2319ab95d3b338e6db49aa21ae.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8dcd36b-62ff-51dd-911e-391155781896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375328 #edb/ref 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=y+1\\,⟹\\,y=x-1\n$$\n\nThe function $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/15/15f9c5d526747b80766a8a3250fa2e705d5d7d9561c52e08492b7a18f1483eb6.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $y=x-1.$ So, we have $\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375329 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "06791450-79a7-57ff-982c-0a9c0c48c94a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375330 #edb/ref 1080 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le y+1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375330 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375330 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "94d1678f-c40b-5af3-9c01-5e44121bc63a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375331 #edb/ref 1080 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375331 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375331 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "84793cb4-e8f5-